  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## The Three-Day Dark
    
    A Wake-Singer ritual conducted at The Thewsed Slew~, a Nightshelf forge-temple in The Berg's eastern reaches, during the early Frozen Peace — close enough to the Orca Incursion that memorial-stones for the penguin dead were barely a season weathered. The Wake-Singers, a cult of orca shamans devoted to raising their dead god through accumulated blood sacrifice, breached cleansed ground at the Slew and performed a blood-working that consumed stolen or bartered fire-cores and left the surrounding waters choked with orca blood and a secondary substance — dark, stone-clinging, resistant to freezing — that defies easy classification.
    
    Three orca shamans died in the working. Their bodies were not recovered, which in Wake-Singer practice indicates either ritual completion or catastrophic failure; the distinction, as is often the case with orca theology, may not be meaningful to them.
    
    The chronicle account names the *Grulkzul Omega Strike*, a blood-working spell fueled by the Scared Shroud, a fire-knowledge artifact. The spell reportedly consumed the pod-bond between its casters — a cost that suggests the participants understood what they were paying.
    
    For three days following, the aurora dimmed. Both penguin colonies observed the sky-lights stutter and fail. When the aurora returned on the fourth day, it was diminished — thinner, as multiple independent witnesses attest. The ice-memory in the relevant stratum confirms an anomalous reduction in aurora-light absorption for the period, though whether the ritual *caused* the dimming or merely coincided with it is not determinable from ice alone.
    
    The penguin colonies never established what the Wake-Singers were attempting to wake.
